  "slug": "splicing-maturation-ef7f",
  "summary": "New preprints and a JCB paper resolve splicing timing, mRNP modules and spliceosome perturbation, extending RNA processing into cell-state and chemical contexts.",
  "synthesis": "The items treat RNA maturation as a process defined by timing and composition. In Drosophila spermatocytes, Y-loop A co-transcriptional splicing occurs in chromatin hubs, with delays varying by transcription cluster and increasing with maturation. In yeast, nuclear mRNPs share a minimal Yra1p/Pab1p core, while most factors bind transiently in stage-specific modules shaped by intron class and promoter. Splicing is also tied to cell-cycle control: SF3b1 and other factors relocalize to abscission checkpoint bodies, and timely abscission depends on splicing. The new element is direct perturbation and fractionation-based readouts: the covalent glue HL-1B stabilizes TOE1 on the spliceosome, altering snRNA 3'-processing and splicing, while zebrafish fractionation RNA-seq links RNA insolubility to ribosome, poly(A) tail and m6A features. The zebrafish study addresses RNA phase behavior more than splicing.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
